\subsection{Smoothing, staging, and bounds}
\label{sec:adaptation-target-implementation}
The targets in \eqref{eq:adapt-sigma-map}, \eqref{eq:adapt-tau-map}, and
\eqref{eq:adapt-default-rs-law} are bounded before smoothing. Every valid sample
updates candidate parameters according to
\begin{equation}
\widetilde u_k=\widetilde u_{k-1}+
 (1-e^{-h/\tau_u})(u_{\star,k}-\widetilde u_{k-1}),
\quad u\in\{\tau,\sigma_{aw},r_S\}.
\label{eq:adapt-ewma}
\end{equation}
For $\tau$ and $\sigma_{aw}$,
$\tau_u=0.40\clip(T_{\rm sea},0.5,6)\,\mathrm{s}$; for $r_S$,
$\tau_u$ is $1.5\tau_\star$. All horizons are finally guarded in
$[0.05,35]\,\mathrm{s}$. The discrepancy-based
horizon-shortening term is disabled.
\label{eq:adapt-ema-sea-time}\label{eq:adapt-RS}
The applied prior amplitude is at least
$\max(0.05\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}},\sigma_{\rm nf,B})$; this prior floor is
distinct from the physical variance floor used in the regularizer target.

When an approximately \SI{0.1}{s} activation timer expires at sample $k$,
the candidate tuple is marked pending and committed at the beginning of sample $k+1$,
before that sample's innovation:
\begin{equation}
\vartheta_k=(\tau_k,\sigma_{aw,k},\vct r_{S,k},T_{S,k})\in\mathcal F_{k-1}.
\label{eq:adapt-one-sample-staging}
\end{equation}
The active MEKF schedule is held between activations: this is a sample-and-hold schedule, not a parameter update every \SI{5}{ms}. An independent approximately \SI{0.1}{s} timer performs bounded
positive-semidefinite top-up of the latent-acceleration covariance.

The realized pseudo-update timer uses the applied time scale,
\begin{equation}
T_S=\clip(c_T\tau_{\rm appl},\SI{5}{ms},\SI{150}{ms}),\qquad
c_T=\frac{\SI{15}{ms}}{\SI{1.1}{s}}.
\label{eq:engineering-pseudo-cadence-implementation}
\end{equation}
It receives the smoothed SpectralMSE standard deviation directly,
$r_{S,\rm filter}=r_{S,\rm appl}$, followed by the axis factors in
\eqref{eq:fus-rs-anisotropy}.
\label{eq:engineering-rs-filter-implementation}
No additional reference-cadence factor is applied. Here $T_{S,\min}=\SI{5}{ms}$ and $T_{S,\max}=\SI{150}{ms}$.
Table~\ref{tab:implementation-gates} summarizes the runtime defaults;
these safeguards are not selected from scored reference errors.
